soc: sunxi: h713-arisc: wait for every fragment's copy, not for the classifier

0091e gave SetEDIDVersion and PullHotPlug RESET an effect wait because their
handlers re-read the scratch behind a log call and the follower's probe lands
in that window. Q13 section 4 D1 called UpdateEDID "the lucky one": its copy
loop starts a handful of instructions after the classifier with no print in
between, so the eight fragments had always arrived byte for byte. Lucky is
not immune. On 22.09.2026 three hdmirx module swaps in a row put the driver
back-to-back with itself and the published block read

  00 ff ff ff ff ff ff e1 fd c5 7f ...

- bytes 0..6 of fragment 0 correct, bytes 7.. replaced - and the attached PC
read no EDID at all and fell back to 848x480. A full `echo edid > cmd`
repaired it (dev21, "DDC block corrupted by rapid module swaps"). That is the
same defect Q13 reported as V1, on the one sub-command that had got away with
it: arisc_wait_handler() is satisfied at 0x11680, when the classifier has
copied the payload into the scratch, while the handler's own copy loop
0x12538..0x12554 is still reading that scratch 64 bytes at a time, and the
next fragment's arisc_arm_handler_probe() writes the complement of its own
expected scratch over all 65 bytes.

The byte to poll is bEdidDataReady, g_Data + 2 (0x1723e, resolved by 0091a as
a->off.edid_ready), and it proves the copy rather than hinting at it. The
store at 0x1256c is the delay slot of 0x12568, the branch that ends the
handler, so it is reached only after the copy loop has exited; and the value
it writes is r3, the very counter the loop counted down from 0x40 to 0
(0x12530 loads it, 0x1253c decrements, 0x1254c tests). The byte reading 0
therefore means all 64 l.sb at 0x12540 have run. Fragment 7 continues at
0x12570, stores 1 and calls the publish routine at 0x12608, which is what
CheckEDIDUpdateStatus reports and what this driver already judges.

0 is also what the byte held before fragments 1..6, so the poll would answer
at once and prove nothing. It is seeded with 0xff first - a value no handler
writes - which is the rule arisc_arm_handler_probe() already applies to the
scratch and arisc_hdmi_set_portmap() to the port records. The seed is safe in
that window: the only readers of the byte are the output routine (0x11d18 in
the gate helper, 0x11da4/0x11dd8 in the publish) and CheckEDIDUpdateStatus's
payload at 0x125c0, and all of them run from a command handler - fragment 7,
SetEDIDVersion, HostHDMIMAP, ResetEDIDModule - none of which can be in flight
while arisc_hdmi_set_edid() holds the lock. A timeout writes 0 back, which is
the truthful "no complete store", and fails the upload with -ETIMEDOUT:
sending the next fragment onto an unfinished copy is the corruption this
patch exists to prevent, and HANDLER_TIMEOUT_MS is 500 ms, so a timeout means
the firmware is wedged, not that the bench is slow.

Not done, and why: the per-fragment destination itself (0x15cdc + frag * 64,
the two 256-byte EDID variants the publish chooses between at 0x11e1c/0x11e24)
would be the most direct readback, but it is initialised data, not BSS, so
0091a neither resolves it nor could arisc_in_bss() accept it, and a poll
against bytes that may already be there is no proof without poisoning the
firmware's own store. The bounded DDC readback via arisc_hdmi_get_edid() was
the other candidate; it needs an unlocked variant, it sees only the one
variant a port is served, and the publish rewrites two of its bytes (the
physical address at 0x11bcc, the extension checksum at 0x11c00), so a
byte-for-byte comparison against what was sent is not available there either.
Both are named in the report; neither is needed once nothing is sent while a
copy is running.

What the HY310 sees: one upload at probe, the same eight UpdateEDID commands
with the same payloads in the same order, the same SetEDIDVersion guard
behind them and the same CheckEDIDUpdateStatus - the mailbox traffic is byte
for byte what it was. What is new is eight SRAM writes to one byte the
sequence overwrites anyway (ResetEDIDModule leaves it 1 at 0x121b8, fragment
7 leaves it 1 at 0x12570) and up to eight waits that the measured handler
finishes inside one poll interval.

Follow-up to 0091/0091a/0091b/0091c/0091d/0091e, which stay byte-identical.

Tested on the HY310: not yet. The device proof is the swap sequence of
22.09.2026 - swap the arisc module, then three hdmirx modules in a row - after
which `sed -n '1p' /sys/kernel/debug/h713-arisc/edid-port0` must still read
000: 00 ff ff ff ff ff ff 00 5e 78 ..., with no "the 64-byte copy did not
finish" line in dmesg. Before this patch the same sequence gave 00 ff ff ff
ff ff ff e1 fd c5 7f.

diff -ruN a/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c b/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c
--- a/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/sunxi/sun50i-h713-arisc.c
@@ -214,6 +214,7 @@
 #define EDID_FRAGMENTS		8
 #define EDID_FRAGMENT_LEN	64
 #define EDID_EXT_OFF		128	/* where the two blocks start to differ */
+#define EDID_READY_PROBE	0xff	/* neither 0 nor 1: no handler leaves it */
 
 /* PullHotPlug, firmware case 7 */
 #define HPD_RESET_COUNTER	84	/* ticks the RESET case loads          */
@@ -1771,6 +1772,48 @@
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(arisc_hdmi_edid_mask);
 
+/*
+ * The fragment handler carries the race 0091e closed for SetEDIDVersion, only
+ * without a log call to widen it: 0x124d4 picks the destination, the loop at
+ * 0x12538..0x12554 copies 64 bytes out of the scratch, and the next command's
+ * arisc_arm_handler_probe() writes the complement of its own expected scratch
+ * over all 65 bytes. After three hdmirx swaps in a row the published block
+ * read 00 ff ff ff ff ff ff e1 fd c5 7f, bytes 7.. of fragment 0 replaced by
+ * the follower's pattern, and the source read no EDID at all (dev21).
+ *
+ * The byte to poll is bEdidDataReady, g_Data + 2, and it is a proof of the
+ * copy rather than a hint: the store at 0x1256c sits in the delay slot of
+ * 0x12568, the branch that ends the handler, so it is reached only once the
+ * copy loop has exited -- and the value it writes is r3, the loop counter the
+ * loop itself counted down from 64 to 0 (0x1253c/0x12544/0x1254c). Seeing it
+ * means all 64 l.sb at 0x12540 have run. Fragment 7 then adds 0x12570, which
+ * stores 1 and publishes. Q13 section 4 D1, the listing in section 1.
+ *
+ * 0 is also what the byte held before fragments 1..6, so it would answer at
+ * once; the seed makes the answer an edge, the same rule
+ * arisc_arm_handler_probe() applies to the scratch and arisc_hdmi_set_portmap()
+ * to the port records. 0xff is safe in that window because the only reader is
+ * the output routine (0x11d18, 0x11da4), and that runs from fragment 7 and
+ * from SetEDIDVersion/HostHDMIMAP -- none of which is in flight here, the
+ * lock being held. A timeout puts the byte back to 0, the truthful "no
+ * complete store", and fails the upload: sending the next fragment onto an
+ * unfinished copy is the corruption this patch is about.
+ */
+static bool arisc_wait_fragment(struct h713_arisc *a, unsigned int frag)
+{
+	u8 want = frag == EDID_FRAGMENTS - 1 ? EDID_DATA_READY : 0;
+
+	if (arisc_wait_effect(a, a->off.edid_ready, want))
+		return true;
+
+	dev_warn(a->dev,
+		 "UpdateEDID fragment %u: bEdidDataReady [0x%05x] reads %u, not %u -- the 64-byte copy did not finish\n",
+		 frag, a->off.edid_ready,
+		 readb(sram_at(a, a->off.edid_ready)), want);
+	writeb(0, sram_at(a, a->off.edid_ready));
+	return false;
+}
+
 /*
  * Upload the EDID. Both versions go up together and in this order because
  * the firmware only publishes anything once fragment 7 has arrived: every
@@ -1799,14 +1842,20 @@
 	for (frag = 0; frag < EDID_FRAGMENTS; frag++) {
 		const u8 *p = bytes + frag * EDID_FRAGMENT_LEN;
 
+		writeb(EDID_READY_PROBE, sram_at(a, a->off.edid_ready));
 		ret = arisc_send(a, SUB_UPDATE_EDID, frag, p[0], p + 1,
 				 EDID_FRAGMENT_LEN - 1);
 		if (ret) {
+			writeb(0, sram_at(a, a->off.edid_ready));
 			dev_err(a->dev,
 				"UpdateEDID Fragment %u nicht verarbeitet (%d)\n",
 				frag, ret);
 			goto out;
 		}
+		if (!arisc_wait_fragment(a, frag)) {
+			ret = -ETIMEDOUT;
+			goto out;
+		}
 	}
 
 	/*
